Spec: specs/001-core-and-anthropic-passthrough/spec.md (AC27, AC28, AC29, AC38, AC47)
ADR: docs/decisions/0003-reverseproxy-with-rewrite-as-proxy-engine.md
Research: Q11 (client_body 400), Q17 (Set, not Add, for Content-Type), Q19 (new, open: a half-close cancels the inbound context)
Why this approach: ErrorHandler classifies from the request-body watcher, which Rewrite now wraps around pr.Out.Body after its five steps (ContentLength untouched), then errors.As net.Error Timeout, then 502. It never logs or formats err. ErrorLog is logging.StdLog(log). The body branch comes before the timeout branch, as plan.md orders it (tasks.md's T10 line lists timeout first): a client-side read failure stays the client's fault whatever the transport wraps it in; no current test tells the two orders apart.
Alternatives rejected: a real connect-timeout via a full accept backlog (thousands of sockets, syncookie-dependent); the test sets ConnectTimeout to 1ns against a never-answering listener, so the dialer's deadline has passed before it connects and a dial that ignored it would hang the test.
Trade-off / known limit: building this showed net/http cancels the request context when a client half-closes after a short body (the connection's EOF), contrary to plan.md step 2. T10 has no context branch, so its 400 holds; T11's context-first branch would turn that case into a 499. Recorded as Q19 (open), T11 marked (blocked: Q19), plan.md step 2 corrected.
Helpers: startGatewayWith/upstreamConfig (per-test timeouts), rawUpstream (a raw listener that hangs or drops), checkNoLeaksAtEnd (the plan's goroutine-profile stack scan, registered first so it runs after every other cleanup; flags stacks through internal/core or a test-server handler). rawAt now sets a 10s connection deadline so a silent gateway fails rather than hangs.
Verified by: TestProxy_GatewayErrorStatus, TestProxy_NoRetry, TestProxy_MalformedClientBody400 and TestAccessLog_GatewayErrorField first failed for the right reasons (no envelope or header, 502 for timeouts and malformed bodies, no gateway_error); the leak check was proved by a throwaway test that found the stuck proxy goroutine mid-request (not committed); go test -race -count=5 ./internal/core; make verify.
